# The Origin of the Northern Lights

Long ago, when the world was young, the Sky Spirit had a daughter named Aurora who loved to dance. Each night, she would twirl and leap across the heavens in a gown woven from starlight, but no one below could see her beauty.

Aurora grew lonely dancing in darkness, unseen by the world. She wept silver tears that fell toward the earth, and where they touched the air, they froze into shimmering crystals of ice.

The Earth Spirit, hearing her sorrow, took pity on the lonely dancer. He gathered the colors of the world—the green of spring forests, the blue of deep oceans, the red of sunset clouds, the purple of mountain twilight, and the gold of autumn leaves. These he breathed into the frozen tears, transforming them into ribbons of living light.

Now, when Aurora dances in the far northern sky, her movements send these colorful ribbons swirling and flowing across the darkness. The people below can finally witness her graceful performance, and she dances with joy, knowing she is no longer alone.

And that is why, on cold winter nights, the northern lights dance across the sky—Aurora's eternal performance, painted in the colors of the earth.
